Lit - a portable Perl reimplementation of LLVM's lit and FileCheck
=================================================================

This distribution provides two test-suite tools modelled on LLVM's `lit`
(the LLVM Integrated Tester) and `FileCheck`, written in pure Perl with no
non-core dependencies.

The point of the exercise is portability.  The LLVM originals need Python
and a POSIX shell; neither can be relied on outside Unix.  Everything here
runs on OpenVMS - VAX, Alpha, I64 and x86-64 - as well as Tru64 and Linux.

Concretely, that meant four decisions:

  * The code is written to Perl 5.6 rules.  No //, say, state, named
    captures, or post-5.8 core modules, because OpenVMS VAX tops out at
    Perl 5.8.

  * RUN: lines are parsed and executed by an internal shell, so no
    /bin/sh is required.  Pipelines are staged through temporary files
    rather than real pipes, which behaves identically on hosts without a
    usable fork() and cannot deadlock.

  * A large set of Unix commands - including FileCheck itself - runs
    in-process.  A suite therefore needs no GNV or other Unix toolkit, and
    the per-CHECK subprocess cost that would dominate a VMS run disappears.

  * Paths are held in Unix syntax internally and converted with
    VMS::Filespec only when handed to a foreign command.


Installing
----------

Unix, Linux, Tru64:

    perl Makefile.PL
    make
    make test
    make install

OpenVMS:

    $ PERL MAKEFILE.PL
    $ MMK                  ! or MMS, or MAKE
    $ MMK TEST
    $ MMK INSTALL

The two programs install as lit.pl and filecheck.pl.  On OpenVMS you will
normally want foreign commands for them:

    $ LIT        :== "PERL DISK$TOOLS:[LIT.BIN]LIT.PL"
    $ FILECHECK  :== "PERL DISK$TOOLS:[LIT.BIN]FILECHECK.PL"

Running from the source tree works too - both scripts find ../lib
themselves, and $LIT_PERL_LIB overrides that if you need it to.


A first test suite
------------------

A suite is a directory containing a config file plus test files:

    mysuite/lit.cfg
    mysuite/hello.test

lit.cfg:

    $config->name('mysuite');
    $config->suffixes('.test');
    $config->test_source_root($config->dir);
    $config->test_exec_root($config->dir . '/_out');

hello.test:

    RUN: echo hello world | FileCheck %s
    CHECK: hello world

Then:

    $ lit.pl -v mysuite
    PASS: mysuite :: hello.test (1 of 1)

    Testing Time: 0.01s

    Total Discovered Tests: 1
      Passed:                 1 (100.00%)


Configuration files
-------------------

lit's own config files are Python, which is exactly the dependency this
distribution exists to avoid, so here they are Perl.  A config file is
evaluated as the body of a sub receiving ($config, $lit_config); it is
ordinary Perl, so anything you can compute you can use.

File names are chosen to be legal on ODS-2, which allows only one dot:

    litsite.cfg   or lit.site.cfg     generated by a build system
    lit.cfg       or litcfg.pl        the suite config, at the suite root
    litlocal.cfg  or lit.local.cfg    per-directory refinement

Searching for a suite walks upwards from each path named on the command
line until one of these appears; that directory becomes the suite root.

$config methods:

    name($str)                  suite name shown in results
    dir()                       directory holding this config file
    suffixes('.cob', '.pli')    which files are tests
    add_suffix(...)
    excludes('Output', ...)     directory/file names to skip
    add_exclude(...)
    test_source_root($dir)      where tests are read from
    test_exec_root($dir)        where scratch output is written
    add_feature('vms', ...)     features REQUIRES:/UNSUPPORTED: can test
    remove_feature(...)
    has_feature($f)
    features()                  arrayref of the current feature names
    add_substitution($pat, $rep)  $pat is a string or a qr//
    env('PATH')                 read a variable in the test environment
    env('PATH', $value)         set one
    unsupported(1)              mark the whole directory unsupported
    pipefail(0)                 a pipeline reports its last stage only
    timeout($seconds)           per-test time limit
    recursive(0)                do not descend into subdirectories
    clone()                     used internally for local configs

$lit_config methods:

    param($name, $default)      value of --param NAME=VALUE
    set_param($name, $value)
    params()                    the whole hashref
    is_vms() is_windows() is_unix()
    host_os() host_arch()
    host_features()             the features added automatically
    load_config($config, $path) pull in another config file
    note($msg) warning($msg) error($msg) fatal($msg)

Every suite starts with a set of host features so that tests can say
REQUIRES:/UNSUPPORTED: without the suite detecting anything itself:

    system-openvms openvms vms       (or system-linux, system-tru64, ...)
    vax alpha ia64 x86_64 x86 aarch64
    host-endian-little host-endian-big
    int64

A worked OpenVMS config:

    $config->name('brcob');
    $config->suffixes('.cob', '.pli');
    $config->test_source_root($config->dir);
    $config->test_exec_root($lit_config->param('build', $config->dir) . '/_out');

    my $compiler = $lit_config->param('brcob', 'SYS$SYSTEM:BRCOB.EXE');
    $config->add_substitution('%brcob', $compiler);
    $config->add_feature('have-cics') if $lit_config->param('cics', 0);

and then

    $ lit.pl --param build=DISK$SCRATCH:[BUILD] --param cics=1 [.TEST]


Test file directives
--------------------

Directives are recognised anywhere in a line, so they sit inside whatever
comment syntax the file already uses.

    RUN: <command>          a command to run; the test fails at the first
                            RUN line that exits non-zero.  A line ending
                            in \ continues onto the next RUN: line.
    REQUIRES: <expr>        skip unless the expression is true
    UNSUPPORTED: <expr>     skip if the expression is true
    XFAIL: <expr>           the test is expected to fail (XFAIL: * always)
    ALLOW_RETRIES: <n>      retry a failing test up to n times
    DEFINE: %{name} = ...   define a substitution for later RUN lines
    REDEFINE: %{name} = ... replace one
    END.                    stop scanning the file here

Expressions take feature names combined with &&, ||, ! and parentheses;
{{regex}} matches any available feature, * is always true, and a bare
comma-separated list means ||.

    REQUIRES: vms && !vax
    UNSUPPORTED: {{system-(linux|darwin)}}

Substitutions available in RUN lines:

    %s   %S   %p        test file, its directory, its directory
    %t   %T             scratch file base, scratch directory
    %basename_s  %basename_t
    %{pathsep}          ':' or ';'
    %%                  a literal percent sign

As with lit on Windows, %s and friends give the host's *native* path
syntax while %/s gives the forward-slash form.  On OpenVMS that is the
difference between DISK:[DIR]FILE.EXT and /disk/dir/file.ext - use %s when
handing a path to a native tool and %/s inside a builtin.

State set by one RUN line (cd, export) persists into the next.  Each test
runs with its working directory set to its own Output directory.


The internal shell
------------------

Supported: words with '...', "..." and backslash quoting; the operators
| || && ; and a leading !; the redirections < > >> N> N>> N>&M &> &>>;
and glob expansion of unquoted words.

Redirections are applied left to right as a shell does, so "> out 2>&1"
and "2>&1 > out" differ in the usual way.

Deliberately unsupported, and diagnosed rather than silently mis-run:
subshells, background &, here-documents, $VAR expansion and backquotes.
lit itself does not expand $VAR either; use substitutions instead.

Builtin commands, all implemented in Perl:

    :  true  false  echo  printf  cat  pwd  cd  export  unset  env
    basename  dirname  mkdir  rmdir  rm  touch  cp  mv  ln
    head  tail  sort  uniq  wc  grep  sed  diff  test  [
    not  count  FileCheck

Notes:

  * FileCheck runs in-process, which is the single biggest win on VMS.
  * `not` inverts the exit status; `not --crash` requires abnormal
    termination.
  * `sed` supports -n, -e, -E/-r and the s/// d p q commands with
    line-number or /regex/ addresses.  Patterns are POSIX BRE by default
    (so \( groups) and ERE under -E.
  * `ln` copies, because ODS-2 has no links.
  * A builtin is preferred over an external program of the same name.


FileCheck
---------

Directives: CHECK, CHECK-NEXT, CHECK-SAME, CHECK-NOT, CHECK-DAG,
CHECK-LABEL, CHECK-EMPTY and CHECK-COUNT-<n>.

Pattern syntax:

    {{regex}}              embedded regular expression
    [[NAME:regex]]         define a string variable
    [[NAME]]               use one
    [[$NAME:regex]]        a global, surviving --enable-var-scope
    [[@LINE]] [[@LINE+n]]  the directive's own line number
    [[#NAME:]]             define a numeric variable
    [[#NAME:expr]]         define it, asserting the match equals expr
    [[#expr]]              match a number equal to expr
    [[#%.8X,NAME:]]        with an explicit format (u d x X, .N)

Numeric expressions take literals (decimal or 0x hex), numeric variables,
@LINE, + and -, and the functions add sub mul div min max.

A variable is not available until its own match completes, so a use must
appear on a later directive than the definition - the same rule as LLVM's
FileCheck.

Options: --check-prefix, --check-prefixes, --comment-prefixes,
--input-file, --strict-whitespace, --match-full-lines, --ignore-case,
--implicit-check-not, --allow-empty, --enable-var-scope,
--allow-unused-prefixes, -DNAME=VALUE, -D#NAME=VALUE, --dump-input,
--dump-input-context.

Embedded regexes are Perl regular expressions.  That is a superset of the
POSIX ERE that LLVM's FileCheck accepts, so existing patterns work, but a
pattern written here may not port back.


Known limitations
-----------------

  * Per-test timeouts need a real fork(), so --timeout is a no-op on
    OpenVMS.  Everything else works there.

  * -j runs tests in parallel only where fork() is available; on OpenVMS
    the run is serial regardless.

  * The DCL backend builds a command procedure per external command, so
    very long argument lists can exceed DCL's line limits.  Prefer a
    response file, or a wrapper .COM, for pathological cases.

  * Test formats other than ShTest (RUN: lines) are not implemented.


